# TypeScript

- Explicit types for function parameters, return types, and variable declarations
- Prefer `interface` over `type` when possible
- Use `readonly` for immutable data structures and result objects
- Prefer type guards over type assertions (`as`)
- Use generic constraints to make types more specific when generics are needed
- No `any`; use `unknown` and narrow it
- Prefer `undefined` for absent values (see `undefined-over-null` rule)
- External/API contract interfaces end with `Dto` (e.g. `NbrbRateDto`) — not `Response`, `Request`, `Payload`, etc. Domain models keep plain names (`NbrbUsdRate`)

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
interface NbrbRateApiResponse { ... }
const data = await res.json() as NbrbRateApiResponse;

// ✅ GOOD
interface NbrbRateDto { ... }
const data: unknown = await res.json();
if (!isNbrbRateDto(data)) {
  throw new Error('Unexpected response');
}
```
